# Security Checklist

Detailed controls that extend the always-on `engineering-security` principles. Library names are examples; use your stack's equivalent.

## Input validation

- Validate structured input with a schema library (e.g. Zod, Pydantic, JSON Schema, FluentValidation, validator crates)
- Enforce length and size limits on every field, body, and upload
- Uploads: allowlist types, check content (magic bytes) not just extension, cap size, store outside the web root with generated names
- Prevent path traversal: resolve and verify paths stay inside an allowed base directory
- Avoid unsafe deserialisation of untrusted data (native object formats, YAML with tags, `pickle`)

```sql
-- Good: parameterised
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1
-- Bad: concatenation
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '${id}'
```

Same rule for shell commands (argument arrays, no shell interpolation), LDAP, XPath, and template engines.

## Authentication

- Prefer a proven identity provider or framework (OIDC/OAuth 2.x) over hand-rolled auth
- Passwords: Argon2id (or scrypt/bcrypt with a strong work factor); check against breached-password lists
- Offer MFA and passkeys/WebAuthn; require MFA for privileged operations
- Access tokens short-lived (≈ 15 min); validate signature, algorithm, issuer, audience, and expiry; reject `alg: none`
- Session/refresh cookies: `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite`; rotate session ID on login and privilege change
- Rate limit and lock out progressively on login, reset, and OTP endpoints; use constant-time comparison for secrets
- Generic responses for login/reset to avoid account enumeration

## Authorisation

- Enforce on the server for every request; never rely on hidden UI or client checks
- Check ownership/tenant of the specific resource (prevents IDOR); default deny
- Map roles/attributes to explicit permissions; centralise policy checks
- Re-check authorisation on background jobs and webhooks acting on behalf of users

## Data protection

- Encrypt sensitive data at rest (e.g. AES-256-GCM via platform/KMS) and in transit (TLS 1.2+, prefer 1.3; HSTS for web)
- Keys in a KMS/secrets manager; rotate; separate per environment
- Use a CSPRNG for tokens, IDs, and nonces
- Minimise collection and retention; define deletion paths for personal data

## XSS

- Rely on framework auto-escaping; encode for the exact output context (HTML, attribute, JS, URL)
- Sanitise any user HTML with a maintained sanitiser (e.g. DOMPurify); never pass raw user content to `innerHTML`-style sinks
- Reject `javascript:` and `data:` URLs from user input
- Set a strict `Content-Security-Policy` (nonces/hashes, no `unsafe-inline`)

## CSRF

- `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` cookies as baseline
- Anti-CSRF tokens (or verified `Origin`/`Sec-Fetch-Site`) for state-changing requests with cookie auth
- Never change state on `GET`

## SSRF

- Allowlist outbound destinations (scheme `https`, host, port)
- Resolve DNS and block private, loopback, link-local, and cloud metadata addresses (e.g. `169.254.169.254`); re-check after redirects
- Route user-influenced fetches through an egress proxy with network policy

## Security headers

| Header | Value |
|--------|-------|
| `Content-Security-Policy` | Restrict script, style, connect, frame-ancestors |
| `Strict-Transport-Security` | `max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` |
| `X-Content-Type-Options` | `nosniff` |
| `Referrer-Policy` | `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` |
| `Permissions-Policy` | Disable unused features (camera, geolocation, …) |
| `X-Frame-Options` | `DENY` (or CSP `frame-ancestors 'none'`) |

Do not rely on the deprecated `X-XSS-Protection`. Restrict CORS to known origins; never reflect arbitrary `Origin` with credentials.

## Errors and exceptional conditions

- Consistent error envelope (e.g. RFC 9457 Problem Details) with no stack traces or internals
- Catch at the right level; fail closed; roll back partial transactions
- Handle timeouts, resource exhaustion, and malformed input without crashing or leaking state

## Logging and alerting

- Log security events: login success/failure, access denied, privilege and credential changes, admin actions
- Structured logs with correlation IDs; mask sensitive fields at the sink; protect logs from tampering
- Alert on anomalies (brute force, spikes in denials, impossible travel) — logs nobody watches do not count

## Supply chain and integrity

- Lockfiles committed; versions pinned; run `npm audit` / `pip-audit` / `cargo audit` / `dotnet list package --vulnerable` / OSV-Scanner in CI
- Generate an SBOM; prefer packages with provenance/signatures; watch for typosquats
- Pin CI actions to commit SHAs; least-privilege CI tokens; protect release branches
- Verify integrity of updates, plugins, and deserialised data (signatures, checksums, SRI for CDN scripts)

## OWASP Top 10:2025 map

| Risk | Key mitigations |
|------|-----------------|
| A01 Broken Access Control (incl. SSRF) | Server-side, resource-level checks; default deny; egress allowlist |
| A02 Security Misconfiguration | Hardened defaults; no debug in prod; strict CORS and headers |
| A03 Software Supply Chain Failures | Lockfiles, scanning, SBOM, pinned CI actions, provenance |
| A04 Cryptographic Failures | Platform crypto, TLS, KMS-managed keys, no MD5/SHA-1 |
| A05 Injection | Parameterised queries, argument arrays, output encoding |
| A06 Insecure Design | Threat modelling, abuse cases, rate limits on business flows |
| A07 Authentication Failures | MFA/passkeys, strong hashing, token validation, rate limiting |
| A08 Software or Data Integrity Failures | Signed artifacts, safe deserialisation, integrity checks |
| A09 Security Logging and Alerting Failures | Security event logs, tamper protection, actionable alerts |
| A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions | Fail closed, safe error responses, resource limits |

## Review checklist

- [ ] Schema validation and size limits at every entry point
- [ ] No concatenated SQL/shell/template input
- [ ] AuthN on non-public routes; resource-level AuthZ (no IDOR)
- [ ] Tokens/cookies configured securely; auth endpoints rate-limited
- [ ] Outbound fetches allowlisted; metadata/private IPs blocked
- [ ] Security headers and CSP set; CORS restricted
- [ ] Errors fail closed and reveal nothing internal
- [ ] Security events logged and alertable; no sensitive data logged
- [ ] Dependencies locked and scanned; CI actions pinned
